  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"123\" data-end=\"353\"><strong data-start=\"123\" data-end=\"153\">Cholesterol in body fluids</strong> (e.g., pleural, peritoneal, or pericardial fluid) is measured to help <strong data-start=\"224\" data-end=\"274\">differentiate between exudates and transudates</strong>&mdash;a key step in evaluating fluid accumulation due to various medical conditions.</p>\n<p data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"391\">This test is particularly useful in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"510\">\n<li data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"433\">\n<p data-start=\"395\" data-end=\"433\"><strong data-start=\"395\" data-end=\"424\">Pleural effusion analysis</strong> (common)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"477\">\n<p data-start=\"436\" data-end=\"477\"><strong data-start=\"436\" data-end=\"477\">Peritoneal (ascitic) fluid evaluation</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"510\">\n<p data-start=\"480\" data-end=\"510\"><strong data-start=\"480\" data-end=\"510\">Pericardial fluid analysis</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"512\" data-end=\"566\">It assists in identifying whether the fluid is due to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"567\" data-end=\"699\">\n<li data-start=\"567\" data-end=\"631\">\n<p data-start=\"569\" data-end=\"631\"><strong data-start=\"569\" data-end=\"592\">Transudative causes</strong> (e.g., heart failure, liver cirrhosis)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"632\" data-end=\"699\">\n<p data-start=\"634\" data-end=\"699\"><strong data-start=\"634\" data-end=\"654\">Exudative causes</strong> (e.g., infections, malignancy, inflammation)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Cholesterol Level (mg/dL)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&lt; 45&ndash;60 mg/dL</strong></td>\n<td>Suggestive of <strong>transudate</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&gt; 60 mg/dL</strong></td>\n<td>Suggestive of <strong>exudate</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Insight</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Malignancy-related effusion</strong></td>\n<td>Often shows high cholesterol in pleural/peritoneal fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tuberculosis</strong></td>\n<td>Common cause of exudative effusion with elevated fluid cholesterol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Empyema or bacterial infection</strong></td>\n<td>Purulent infections can lead to increased cholesterol levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rheumatoid pleuritis / lupus</strong></td>\n<td>Autoimmune conditions with exudative characteristics often elevate cholesterol</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
